Congress plans to vote later today on a  bipartisan compromise budget bill that  will prevent another partial government  shutdown  White House officials believe President  Trump will sign it even though it  doesn't give him the money to build a  border wall the president indicated  yesterday he will find another way to  build it as we review the new proposal  from Congress I can promise you this  I will never waver from my sacred duty  to defend this nation and its people we  will get the job done the wall is very  very on its way Nancy Cordes is on  Capitol Hill Hill rather where the final  plan was revealed overnight Nancy good  morning to you good morning and that  final plan is expected to get  overwhelming support when it goes up for  a vote in the House and Senate today no  one wants to see another shutdown and  they want to send a powerful message to  the president that he should sign this  bill even if it doesn't contain  everything that he wanted the bill does  give the Department of Homeland Security  roughly 25 billion dollars for border  security funding in 2019 that's a modest  increase from 2018 included is nearly  one point four billion dollars for  physical barriers fifty-five new miles  of fencing along the border now that  fence which some Republicans are calling  a wall will be steel packed with  concrete 30 feet tall in some areas 18  feet tall in others and one thing that  won't be in the bill is a measure that  was proposed by Democrats at the last  minute to provide back pay to federal  contractors affected by the government  shutdown the White House signaling that  the president will sign the bill and he  has said repeatedly Norah that he'll  find the rest of the money he wanted  somewhere else all right Nancy looks  like a beautiful morning there in  Washington thank you
 

